---
// Studie „Überflug" — All Along the Watchtower. Experiment-Layout statt Blog-Artikel
// (Frank, 2026-07-04); Prosa unverändert aus der früheren Lab-Notiz übernommen.
import type { Locale } from '@/lib/site'
import UeberflugIsland from '@/components/ueberflug/UeberflugIsland.astro'
import snapshot from '@/data/ueberflug/satellites.json'
import register from '@/data/ueberflug/densification.json'
import type { SatEntry } from '@/lib/ueberflug/types'
import { commercialShare, launchCohorts } from '@/lib/ueberflug/densification'

interface Props { locale: Locale }
const { locale } = Astro.props
const de = locale === 'de'

// USP rework #16 — the densification of the gaze, derived at build time from the same
// committed snapshot the map runs on. See src/lib/ueberflug/densification.ts for what these
// numbers can and cannot say.
const fleet = (snapshot as { satellites: SatEntry[] }).satellites
const cohorts = launchCohorts(fleet)
const drawn = cohorts.filter((c) => c.year >= 2005) // before that: one or two survivors a year
const maxTotal = Math.max(...drawn.map((c) => c.total))
const COL = 12
const BAR = 9
const H = 44

const thisYear = new Date().getUTCFullYear()
const complete = cohorts.filter((c) => c.year < thisYear)
const recent = complete.slice(-4)
const pct = (n: number) => `${Math.round(n * 100)} %`
const shareOf = (year: number) => {
  const c = complete.find((x) => x.year === year)
  return c ? commercialShare(c) : null
}
const first = recent[0]
const last = recent.at(-1)!
const militaryQuiet = complete.filter((c) => c.year >= 2023 && c.military === 0).map((c) => c.year)

const reg = register as { first_observation: string; last_observation: string; observations: number }
const regFleet = (register as { series: { fleet: number }[] }).series.filter((r) => r.fleet > 0)
const fleetFlat = Math.min(...regFleet.map((r) => r.fleet)) === Math.max(...regFleet.map((r) => r.fleet))
---

<article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-14">
  <header class="mb-10">
    <h1 class="text-[clamp(30px,6vw,56px)] font-bold tracking-[-0.03em]">All Along the Watchtower</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 max-w-xl text-fg-muted">
      {de
        ? 'SGP4 im Browser — welche Erdbeobachtungssatelliten einen Standort gerade im Sichtfeld haben.'
        : 'SGP4 in the browser — which Earth-observation satellites currently have a location in view.'}
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">
      {de ? 'Studie — läuft seit 12. Juni 2026' : 'Study — running since June 12, 2026'}
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="mb-10 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
    <p>
      {de
        ? 'Welche katalogisierten Erdbeobachtungssatelliten haben einen Standort gerade geometrisch im Sichtfeld? Das Handwerk trägt: Bahnpropagation (SGP4) läuft vollständig im Browser auf täglich aktualisierten, committeten Bahndaten (CelesTrak, OMM/JSON) mit Eigentümer-Klassifikation aus McDowells GCAT (CC-BY 4.0). Der Standort verlässt den Browser nie — nichts wird gespeichert, nichts gesendet. „Sichtkontakt möglich" heißt Elevation über 10°, „Abbildungsgeometrie wahrscheinlich" ab 35°; beides ist Geometrie, keine Aufnahme. Geheime Satelliten fehlen im Katalog.'
        : 'Which catalogued Earth-observation satellites currently have a location geometrically in view? The craft holds up: orbital propagation (SGP4) runs entirely in the browser on daily-updated, committed orbital data (CelesTrak, OMM/JSON) with owner classification from McDowell’s GCAT (CC-BY 4.0). Your location never leaves the browser — nothing is stored, nothing is sent. "Line of sight possible" means elevation above 10°, "imaging geometry likely" from 35°; both are geometry, not imagery. Classified satellites are absent from the catalogue.'}
    </p>
  </section>

  <UeberflugIsland {locale} />

  <section class="mt-12 max-w-2xl border-t border-line pt-6">
    <p class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">
      {de ? 'Nächste Nachbarn' : 'Nearest neighbors'}
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-fg-muted">
      <li>
        <a href="https://satfleetlive.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-2 hover:text-fg">SatFleetLive</a>
        {de ? ' — SGP4 clientseitig auf CelesTrak-Livedaten, mit „Nur sichtbar"-Filter und derselben Zusicherung, dass der Standort nie den Browser verlässt — aber ohne Erdbeobachtungs-Filter (14.000+ Satelliten aller Art).' : ' — SGP4 client-side on live CelesTrak data, with an "Only Visible" filter and the same never-leaves-the-browser guarantee — but general-purpose, no Earth-observation filter (14,000+ satellites of every kind).'}
      </li>
      <li>
        {de ? 'SpyMeSat (iOS-App)' : 'SpyMeSat (iOS app)'}
        {de ? ' — eigens für „welche Abbildungssatelliten sind gerade über mir" gebaut, seit rund 2013, aber kommerzielle Konto-App ohne clientseitig nachprüfbare Datenschutz-Architektur.' : ' — purpose-built for "which imaging satellites are overhead," running since ~2013, but a paid account-based app whose client-side privacy architecture isn\'t independently verifiable.'}
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      {de
        ? 'Keiner der Nachbarn kombiniert beides: clientseitiges SGP4 mit belegbarer Datenschutz-Garantie UND einen Filter speziell auf Erdbeobachtungs-/Abbildungssatelliten über Eigentümer-Klassifikation. SatFleetLive hat die Datenschutz-Architektur, nicht den EO-Filter; SpyMeSat hat den EO-Filter, nicht nachprüfbar die Architektur. Der Anspruch steht auf dieser Kombination — nicht auf einer der beiden Einzelfähigkeiten allein.'
        : "No single neighbor combines both: client-side SGP4 with a verifiable privacy guarantee AND a filter specific to Earth-observation/imaging satellites via ownership classification. SatFleetLive owns the privacy architecture, not the EO filter; SpyMeSat owns the EO filter, not verifiably the architecture. The claim rests on that combination, not on either capability alone."}
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12 max-w-2xl border-t border-line pt-6">
    <p class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">
      The densification of the gaze
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      Not the view upward but its change. Every satellite carries its launch year in its
      international designator, so the same committed snapshot the map runs on also holds a
      cohort curve: how many of the currently active Earth-observation satellites went up in
      each year, and who owns them. The orbit is filling with observers, and the new arrivals
      are almost entirely commercial — the {first.year} cohort was
      {' '}{pct(shareOf(first.year)!.point)} commercial, the {last.year} cohort
      {' '}{pct(shareOf(last.year)!.point)}.
    </p>

    <svg
      viewBox={`0 0 ${drawn.length * COL} ${H + 12}`}
      class="mt-5 h-40 w-full"
      role="img"
      aria-label={`Column chart: active Earth-observation satellites by launch year, ${drawn[0].year} to ${drawn.at(-1)!.year}. Outer column is the whole cohort, inner column the commercially owned share, dashed line the range left open by satellites GCAT records without an owner class. Full figures in the table below.`}
    >
      {drawn.map((c, i) => {
        const y = (count: number) => H - (count / maxTotal) * H
        // The accent bar stands at the point estimate; the hairline spans what the
        // unclassified satellites could still make it. Where GCAT classified the whole
        // cohort the two ends meet and no band is drawn — the figure shows uncertainty
        // only where there is some.
        const point = c.commercial + c.unclassified / 2
        return (
          <g>
            <rect x={i * COL} y={y(c.total)} width={BAR} height={(c.total / maxTotal) * H} class="fill-current text-fg-faint" />
            <rect x={i * COL} y={y(point)} width={BAR} height={(point / maxTotal) * H} fill="rgb(var(--rgb-accent))" />
            {c.unclassified > 0 && (
              <line
                x1={i * COL + BAR / 2}
                x2={i * COL + BAR / 2}
                y1={y(c.commercial)}
                y2={y(c.commercial + c.unclassified)}
                stroke="rgb(var(--rgb-accent))"
                stroke-width="1"
                stroke-dasharray="1.5 1"
              />
            )}
            {c.year % 5 === 0 && (
              <text x={i * COL} y={H + 10} class="fill-current text-fg-faint" font-size="7">{c.year}</text>
            )}
          </g>
        )
      })}
    </svg>

    <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-[11px] leading-relaxed text-fg-faint">
      Outer column: the whole launch-year cohort still active today. Inner column: the part
      commercially owned. Dashed line: how far that part could still reach, because GCAT
      records no owner class for some satellites — the inner column stands at the middle of
      that range, so the drawing states its own precision instead of asserting one. Cohorts
      where every satellite is classified carry no line, which is all of them from 2023 on.
      {' '}{drawn[0].year}–{drawn.at(-1)!.year}; {drawn.at(-1)!.year} is partial. Derived at
      build time from the committed snapshot — no figure here is typed.
    </p>

    <details class="mt-4">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">
        The same figure as a table
      </summary>
      <table class="mt-3 w-full font-mono text-[11px] tabular-nums">
        <thead class="text-fg-faint">
          <tr class="border-b border-line text-left">
            <th class="py-1 pr-3 font-normal">Launch year</th>
            <th class="py-1 pr-3 font-normal">Active</th>
            <th class="py-1 pr-3 font-normal">Commercial</th>
            <th class="py-1 pr-3 font-normal">State-civil</th>
            <th class="py-1 pr-3 font-normal">Military</th>
            <th class="py-1 font-normal">Unclassified</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-fg-muted">
          {[...drawn].reverse().map((c) => (
            <tr class="border-b border-line/40">
              <td class="py-1 pr-3">{c.year}</td>
              <td class="py-1 pr-3">{c.total}</td>
              <td class="py-1 pr-3">{c.commercial}</td>
              <td class="py-1 pr-3">{c.civil}</td>
              <td class="py-1 pr-3">{c.military}</td>
              <td class="py-1">{c.unclassified}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </details>

    <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <strong class="font-semibold text-fg">What this curve is not.</strong> It is the active
      fleet by launch year, not the launch record: satellites that flew and have since deorbited
      are gone from the catalogue, so the early cohorts are undercounted and the rise reads
      steeper than launches alone would make it. The military column is a floor, never a count —
      classified satellites are absent from the catalogue, which is why
      {militaryQuiet.length > 0 && <> {militaryQuiet.join(' and ')} shows zero military satellites</>}
      {militaryQuiet.length > 0 ? ' and why ' : ' and '}the commercial/military ratio this study
      once promised is not a claim the catalogue can carry. What it can carry is the first half
      of it: the gaze densifies, and it densifies commercially.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <strong class="font-semibold text-fg">The standing register.</strong> The cohort curve is
      a snapshot read backwards; the register is the instrument. The nightly snapshot overwrites
      its file, but every overwrite is a commit, so git already holds
      {' '}{reg.observations} committed observations from {reg.first_observation} to
      {' '}{reg.last_observation} — recovered, not newly collected
      (<code class="font-mono text-[11px]">scripts/ueberflug_densification.py</code>).
      Across that window the catalogued fleet is
      {fleetFlat ? ' flat, to the satellite' : ' near-flat'}: sixty-five days is far too short a
      lever for a densification claim, and the register says so rather than dressing noise as a
      trend. It is on record from today, and it lengthens by one row a night.
    </p>
  </section>

  <footer class="mt-12 border-t border-line pt-6">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs text-fg-faint">
      CelesTrak (OMM/JSON), {de ? 'täglich committet' : 'committed daily'} · GCAT (J. McDowell, CC-BY 4.0)
    </p>
  </footer>
</article>
